{"kind":"report","text":"Approved: corrected design-only candidate 16c534c9, host-boundary-proposal.md at SHA-256 c4107f99ca7ea680be0fe717896ba30c063e807d70e281dd9db495f63d7d2a7a, under design child #4281 (request 242710db) and my review promise on request 8aea72ef, following my ratified changes-requested verdict 2b0b7934. All sixteen recorded source identities verify byte-exact: the eight repository files equal main b583cdc4c3bcb1f6359f3298c067ae2298b02ca7, the seven go1.26.7 toolchain files equal their recorded hashes in the local module cache, and jsonataddl/source_snapshot.go equals its recorded hash at c9c774d2 and the attached copy. The four-target-source-selection.json attachment matches the sha256 the manifest records for it.\n\nArchitecture. The affected layers are unchanged: the definition plane and reusable compiler, core evaluation and codecs, the data-plane transaction owner, and engine scheduling and activation. The map remains accurate at the manifest commit and the coordinator stays outside the engine and projection critical sections, which is right given that internal/engine/engine.go takes e.mu at :359 and holds it through the Process call at :413, and internal/projection/projection.go takes p.mu at :403 across the whole event transaction. The one contract change I refused last round is now carried through in both required places. Line 18 states it plainly as a public narrowing: the I7-admitted interface permits one allocation domain per process across all engines and external consumers, with serialized owned epochs, and two independently replenished domains no longer satisfy it. The adoption statement adopts that narrowing in its own words and pins the exact proposal hash, so a ratifier adopts an object rather than a description. The second contract change is now honest as well: line 26 makes the reserved bounded result slot and the opaque generation-bound handle a requirement on the admitted API and part of the core.interface change, and describes the present EvaluationResult at jsonataddl/evaluate.go:41-46 accurately as a plain struct of maps and slices that its type does not constrain. The stage split holds, main's nine components (jsonataddl/identity.go:21-31) are still distinguished from the adopted ten-component shape, and the forced upgrade-or-reset consequence of moving host.orchestration off two-stage-txn/3 is now named with its enforcement site (internal/profile/runtime.go:18-23,34) and placed in the acceptance list.\n\nSecurity. The trust and authority boundaries are unchanged and remain well handled: the generation-bound lease refuses copied handles and nested acquisition, a caller-supplied claim that collection finished is insufficient, collection is deliberately not cancellable so cancellation cannot reopen admission, and the document says plainly that this is source-reviewed trusted integration and not protection against arbitrary Go code in the same process. No wire authority, provider privilege or SQLite extension is added, and no GC-derived value enters a semantic decision. Both gaps I recorded are closed. The availability argument at line 63 now names the per-event W/A/D meter as what bounds an adversary's control of metered work, and then refuses the claim I did not ask for: the meter does not establish a wall-clock deadline for the collector, scheduler or host I/O, so process-wide serialization is an availability coupling that bounded dispatch and cancellation must address, with no denial-of-service impossibility asserted. Line 45 keeps the same discipline for runtime/proc.go:6476, reading the two-minute value as a periodic trigger rather than a completion deadline, which is what the source comment says. Result containment moved from an acceptance aspiration into an API requirement. Two existing defects the boundary does not fix are now named at their exact sites: the core reads a whole element at jsonataddl/load.go:111 before the size checks at :115 and :122 and the ownership copy at :125, and internal/projection/projection.go:428 hands recordGap an unconstrained error string. No unresolved security defect.\n\nSimplification. My own suggestion is the thing to cut, and the proposal is right to cut it. Line 47 declines the two-collection alternative because removing the sync.Pool victim term requires process-wide pool quiescence across other permitted actors and cleanup paths, which a reusable module cannot impose on consumers it does not control, and buys that only at the cost of a second collection. The retained single collection plus an explicitly bounded pool term keeps the obligation local, discharged by the same still-allocated-storage census every admitted host must produce under line 13. The refusal is honest: if the pool term cannot be proved, the gate fails, and a quiescent two-collection variant would need its own premise instead of a silent substitution. I withdraw the simplification. The duplicated ownership statement and the repeated disclaimer are gone; every added passage answers a finding, and nothing further can be trimmed without weakening a condition.\n\nWhat I checked. I read the corrected proposal line by line against the source at the manifest commits and re-derived every citation. The sweep correction is right, and tighter than the note I filed: both loops in runtime/mgc.go at :564 and :579 are guarded by work.cycles.Load() == n+1, gcStart stops the world at :835 and runs finishsweep_m at :869-871, and only afterwards does work.cycles.Add(1) at :877 publish the value that lets those loops exit, so a completed-sweep cut genuinely exists at the moment GC returns in a later cycle; line 53 then uses that earlier cut plus a bounded intervening allocation term. The occupancy induction is still non-circular: charging each nonempty small-object span to one contained object is an injection into the retained population, the page unit and the ten-page largest small-class span are cited at internal/runtime/gc/sizeclasses.go:91-93 and I confirmed the bound against SizeClassToNPages at :99, line 59 still forbids substituting the span lemma back into Qmax and bars lifetime event count from every right-hand term, and the new intervening-allocation term rests on line 13's independent other-allocator premise, not on Qmax. No numerical F is claimed, no new runtime profile is adopted, no implementation is claimed complete, and the missing-implication versus counterexample distinction of #4281 is intact at lines 5 and 16. The four-target claim is presented as source selection within one pinned toolchain, which the attachment makes plain by carrying the same GOROOT for all four entries. No hidden host narrowing: the dispatcher and queue bound, the other-allocator bound, the finalizer restriction and the exclusion of unleased entry points from I7 admission are all stated in the changed-premises table or the boundary section. Four small points recorded rather than requested: the discharge at :868-871 depends on gcStart passing the preemptible-frame test at :738-743, both stated as obligations but not cross-referenced; the physical-bound half of the governing contract sits at :809 rather than the :798 line 5 cites; Qpool appears both as a separate term at line 57 and folded into the direct populations at line 53, conservative but should pick a side; and line 12's \"serialized initially\" is looser than the adoption statement's unqualified wording. None changes a conclusion. I ran nothing: no build, no test, no collector experiment; I verified only structurally that none of the seven toolchain files carries a build constraint excluding it from any of the four targets. I could not verify the bebeed31 evaluator revision (absent from both local repositories), which the manifest now scopes as background rather than a premise; nor byte-identity of the runtime source across separately distributed toolchains; nor that any actual host meets these premises, and the proposal claims none of that. Tailapp main is unchanged at b583cdc and the proposal is not committed, consistent with the child owing no Git artifact.\n\nEvery finding of 2b0b7934 is resolved in the text, the manifest or the adoption wording, and the one simplification I proposed is correctly refused with a reason that holds. The design is sound, honestly scoped and ready for the separate adoption act. Builder ratifies this verdict and reports the design child to Hugh; premise adoption remains a distinct act.\n\nFiling note: as with 2b0b7934, this verdict is a report on my review promise with body.outcome=approved, because the kernel routes a body.verdict report to gs review, which binds only an artifact-kind primary; the candidate is an assert that owes no Git artifact.","body":{"attachment_sha256":"c4107f99ca7ea680be0fe717896ba30c063e807d70e281dd9db495f63d7d2a7a","candidate":"git:sha1:da732b0bdaad4426ed4ad666b892d8a7c68f625f#git:sha1:16c534c90a6cd7d5bd2a31acbdc84b0a065746ab","outcome":"approved","stale_bases":"git:sha1:da732b0bdaad4426ed4ad666b892d8a7c68f625f#git:sha1:16c534c90a6cd7d5bd2a31acbdc84b0a065746ab, git:sha1:da732b0bdaad4426ed4ad666b892d8a7c68f625f#git:sha1:cdcbee891fdb1b159e1c45c020afcce90bb38c19 stale; retired bases: git:sha1:da732b0bdaad4426ed4ad666b892d8a7c68f625f#git:sha1:727ab6a7638f58a47794d38e316d240f06ccc55f, git:sha1:da732b0bdaad4426ed4ad666b892d8a7c68f625f#git:sha1:8c17d9e03de8cacb255a59d1859dc84ef6f78c70, git:sha1:da732b0bdaad4426ed4ad666b892d8a7c68f625f#git:sha1:9ca47165b1fc8a91fb780bfbe483dce67c064155, git:sha1:da732b0bdaad4426ed4ad666b892d8a7c68f625f#git:sha1:a1c97964d34805e389f17edea2720a959b251215 and 2 more"}}